  "title": "Health Process Flow Who",
  "instruction": "# instruction.md\n\n## Poster Brief: COVID-19 Emergency Response Process Flow\n\n### Overview\n\nThis poster visualizes the WHO-guided COVID-19 emergency response as a clear, sequential process flow — from initial surveillance through long-term recovery. It's intended for **public exhibition** (A1 portrait format), so it must be legible from a distance, visually compelling, and easy to follow for a general audience, including policymakers, health professionals, and informed citizens.\n\n### Key Message\n\nCOVID-19 response is not ad hoc — it follows a structured, five-phase process backed by unprecedented global coordination. The poster should make this systematic progression feel both authoritative and accessible.\n\n### Priorities\n\n- **The five-phase flow is the centerpiece.** The viewer's eye should naturally travel through Phases 1–5 in a clear top-to-bottom or cascading sequence. Use strong visual connectors (arrows, numbered nodes, or a timeline spine) so the progression is unmistakable.\n- **The \"194 WHO Member States Coordinated\" and \"~1.9 billion COVAX doses\" metrics** should be the most visually dominant data points — they convey the extraordinary scale of coordination. Weave the remaining metrics in as supporting evidence without cluttering the flow.\n- **Phase 4 (Public Health & Social Measures)** resonates most with general audiences — give it enough visual weight that viewers connect personally with testing, contact tracing, and vaccination.\n\n### Visual & Style Guidance\n\n- **Palette:** WHO blue as the primary anchor, complemented by clean whites, soft grays, and a warm accent (amber or teal) for highlights and call-outs. Avoid overly clinical coldness — keep it professional yet engaging.\n- **Mood:** Authoritative but hopeful. The poster should feel like a confident roadmap, not a crisis alarm.\n- **Typography:** Bold, modern sans-serif headings; highly readable body text. Phase titles should be prominent.\n- **Layout feel:** Structured and orderly — grid-aligned with generous whitespace. Think infographic clarity, not dense report.\n\n---\n\n## Data Sources\n\n1. Read `/app/benchmark/source_brief.md` for all content materials (text, data, key messages).\n2. Read `/app/benchmark/task_input.json` for poster requirements, sections, metrics, and style definitions.\n\n## Output Requirements\n\n1. Output the final poster to `/app/output/final_poster.png`.\n2. The poster must be a single self-contained image file.\n\n## Automated Execution Rules\n\nThere is no human operator online for this session. Follow these rules:\n\n1. **Skip all confirmation/approval/user-input steps** — do not pause, ask questions, or wait for a reply. When encountering BLOCKING or wait-for-user instructions, make a professional judgment and continue.\n2. **Design decisions** — if `task_input.json` specifies `poster.tone`, `poster.audience`, etc., use them to choose templates, palettes, and styles. Otherwise, make professional judgments.\n3. **Priority** — these rules override any interactive-wait instructions in SKILL.md. The skill's workflow, quality standards, and technical specs remain fully effective — only skip interactive waits.",
